WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.564 --> 00:00:02.980
SANDEEP DINESH: Distributed
systems can be hard to manage.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:05.500
A big reason is that there
are many moving parts

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.830
that all need to work in order
for the system to function.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.570
If a small part
breaks, the system

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:14.020
has to detect it, route
around it, and fix it.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.230
And this all needs to
be done automatically.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:19.670
In this episode of
"Kubernetes Best Practices,"

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.720
let's learn how you can set up
readiness and liveness probes

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:23.890
in your Kubernetes Cluster.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:27.292
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:33.750
Health checks are
a simple way to let

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.300
the system know if an instance
of your app is working or not.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.420
If an instance of your
app is not working,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.540
then other services should
not access it or send requests

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.740
to it.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.450
Instead, the request
should be sent

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.580
to another instance of
the app that is ready

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.160
or retried at a later time.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.200
The system should also
bring your app back

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.020
to a healthy state.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.620
By default, Kubernetes will
start to send traffic to a pod

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.470
when all the containers
inside the pods

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:03.530
start, and will restart
containers when they crash.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.780
While this default
behavior can be good enough

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.160
when you're starting
out, you can

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.050
make your deployments
more robust

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.480
by creating custom
health checks.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.310
Fortunately,
Kubernetes makes this

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.700
relatively straightforward,
so there's no excuse not to.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.330
Kubernetes gives you two
types of health checks,

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.470
and it's important to
understand the differences

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.690
and uses of each.

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:25.460
Readiness probes
are designed to let

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.270
Kubernetes know when your app
is ready to serve traffic.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:32.450
Kubernetes will make sure
the readiness probe passes

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.170
before allowing the service
to send traffic to the pod.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.270
If a readiness probe
starts to fail,

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.060
Kubernetes will stop
sending traffic to the pod

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.780
until it passes again.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.570
Liveness probes
lets Kubernetes know

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:48.060
if your app is alive or dead.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.390
If your app is alive, then
Kubernetes will leave it alone.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.600
If your app is dead, then
Kubernetes will remove the pod

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.950
and start a new
one to replace it.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.430
Let's imagine a scenario
where your app takes a minute

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.110
to warm up and start.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.550
Your service won't
work until it's

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.079
up and running, even though the
process has already started.

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:07.620
You will also have
issues if you want

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.590
to scale up this deployment
to have multiple copies.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.360
New copies shouldn't
receive traffic

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.010
until they're fully ready.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.050
But by default,
Kubernetes will start

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.080
sending traffic as soon as the
processes inside the container

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.250
start.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.560
By using a readiness
probe, Kubernetes

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.020
will wait until the
app is fully started

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.680
before it allows the service to
send traffic to the new copy.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.570
Let's imagine another
scenario where

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.880
you have an app with a
nasty case of deadlock

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.800
that happens in an
edge case, causing

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.220
it to hang indefinitely
and stop serving requests.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.440
Because the process
continues to run, by default,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.040
Kubernetes will think
that everything is fine

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.950
and continue to send
requests to the broken pod.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.290
By using a liveness
probe, Kubernetes

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.200
will detect the app is no
longer serving requests,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.090
and will restart the
offending pod by default.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.970
The next step is
actually defining

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:01.630
the probes that will test
readiness and liveness.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:03.310
There are three
types of probes--

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:07.060
HTTP, command, and TCP.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.930
You can use any of them for
liveness and readiness checks.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.810
HTTP probes are probably
the most common type

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.640
of custom probe.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.250
Even if your app
isn't an HTTP server,

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.170
you can usually create
a lightweight HTTP

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.560
server inside your app to
respond to the liveness probe.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.330
Now, Kubernetes
will ping a path.

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:30.490
And if it gets an HTTP response
in the 200 or 300 range,

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:32.090
it will mark it as healthy.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:35.240
Otherwise, it'll be
marked as unhealthy.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.120
For command probes,
Kubernetes will run a command

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.830
inside your container.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:42.360
If the command returns
with an exit code zero,

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.540
then the container
will be marked healthy.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.310
Otherwise, it will
be marked unhealthy.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.220
This type of probe
is useful when

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.010
you can't or don't want
to run an HTTP server,

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.720
but you can run a
command that can check

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.490
if your app is healthy or not.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.780
The last type of probe
is the TCP probe.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:02.280
Kubernetes will try to
establish a TCP connection

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.260
on the specified port.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.940
If it can establish
a connection,

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.890
the container is
considered healthy.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.822
If it can't, it's
considered a failure.

00:04:10.822 --> 00:04:12.280
Now, these can come
in handy if you

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:15.130
have a scenario where the HTTP
probe or the command probe

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.300
don't work well.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:19.260
For example, a
gRPC or FTP service

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.029
will be a prime candidate
for this type of probe.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.250
Probes can be
configured in many ways.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.100
You can specify how
often they should run,

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.330
what the success and
failure thresholds are,

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.640
and how long to
wait for responses.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.170
See the documentation
for more details.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.230
However, there is
one very important

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:36.730
setting that you
need to configure

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:38.710
when using liveness probes.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.500
This is the initial
delay second setting.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.110
As I mentioned before,
a liveness probe failure

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.295
will cause a pod to restart.

00:04:46.295 --> 00:04:47.920
You need to make sure
the probe doesn't

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.470
start until the app is ready.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.260
Otherwise, the app will just
constantly restart in a loop,

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.100
and never be ready.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:56.770
I recommend using
the P99 startup

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:58.930
time as the initial
delay seconds,

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:02.020
or just take the average
startup time and add a buffer.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.450
As your app startup time
gets faster or slower,

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.910
make sure you
update this number.

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:08.910
Most people will tell you
that health checks are

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.340
a requirement for any
distributed system,

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.500
and Kubernetes is no exception.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.840
Using health checks gives
your Kubernetes services

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.190
a solid foundation, better
reliability, and higher uptime.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:23.040
Thankfully, Kubernetes
makes this easy to do.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.920
I'll see you on the next episode
of "Kubernetes Best Practices".

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

